---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Practice"
  description="Applied engagement: training, diplomacy, and institutional work grounded in CAV research — without overclaiming what the research has established."
>
  <PageHeader
    kicker="Applied engagement"
    title="Practice follows standing."
    lede="CAV’s fifth strand is applied work with diplomats, mediators, and institutions. It remains deliberately modest: we will not ship modules that teach people to pronounce a virtue they have not studied."
  />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-12 sm:px-8">
    <article class="measure prose-cav text-[1.05rem]">
      <h2>Relation to research</h2>
      <p>
        Practice is the applied counterpart to the four analytic pillars — Ethno-Ethics,
        Sacred Axiology, Semantic Ethics, and Chronological Ethics. Engagement draws on
        comparative insight only where method and community authority support it. Where
        an entry is still under review, the training is pending.
      </p>

      <h2>Who this is for</h2>
      <p>
        Diplomats, mediators, humanitarian staff, institutional leaders, educators, and
        researchers who already know that a shared vocabulary of “values” sometimes ends
        a conversation that needed to continue — or flattens a moral claim that deserved
        its remainder.
      </p>

      <h2>What engagement will look like</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Briefings.</strong> Short, sourced sessions on a single concept or
          pillar theme, and on the harm of its usual portable English stand-in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Seminars.</strong> Longer work on listening for remainder — the part of
          a moral claim that does not fit the talking points — including Semantic Ethics
          cases where untranslatable virtues carry diplomatic weight.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Institutional reviews.</strong> Reading a mission statement, code of
          conduct, or training deck for flattening language, and proposing sharper
          alternatives only where we have standing to do so.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Field methods.</strong> Practical habits: how to ask, how to wait, how
          to record a term or practice without extracting it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we will not do</h2>
      <p>
        We will not certify cultural competence, sell a national-character profile, or
        train anyone to perform a virtue that is not theirs. We will not overclaim
        completed findings from intended methods. Practice follows research.
      </p>
      <p>
        Organizations that want to think with us while curriculum and methods are being
        written should start on the <a href="/join">Join</a> page.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
